 "answer": "According to the Malaysian Department of Environment’s Air Pollutant Index Management System (APIMS), the Air Pollutant Index (API) reading in **Shah Alam** is **170**, placing it in the **Unhealthy** category.\n\n### **Hourly Trend (Local Time)**\n* **7:00 AM:** 167 (Unhealthy)\n* **8:00 AM:** 168 (Unhealthy)\n* **9:30 AM:** 169 (Unhealthy)\n* **12:00 PM:** **170 (Unhealthy)**\n\n### **What This Means (API 101–200: Unhealthy)**\n* **Health Impact:** Air quality has deteriorated due to regional haze. Sensitive groups (such as children, the elderly, and individuals with asthma or respiratory/heart conditions) may experience more serious health effects, while the general public may begin to experience mild irritation or breathing discomfort.\n* **Recommendations:** Limit prolonged or heavy outdoor physical exertion, wear an N95 mask if you need to be outdoors for extended periods, and keep windows closed to maintain indoor air quality. \n\n*(For real-time hourly updates directly from the Department of Environment, you can check the official [APIMS portal](http://apims.doe.gov.my/).)*",
